<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kantoli</h1><p class="st-meta">441 CE – 563 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sumatran trading kingdom known only from Chinese records of its 5th- and 6th-century embassies, often seen as a forerunner of Srivijaya.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Known only from Chinese dynastic histories, Kantoli sent envoys to the southern Chinese courts in 441, 455, 502, 518 and 563. Historians usually place it in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, around Jambi or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, though its exact site is unknown.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chinese texts describe a Buddhist king who exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth, areca nut and aromatic woods. In 502 a king called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-subhadra">Gautama Subhadra</a> sent tribute, followed by his son Vijayavarman. The embassies suggest a ruler eager for Chinese recognition to attract merchants to his port on the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kantoli disappears from the records after 563. Its role as a Sumatran gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/melayu-kingdom">Melayu Kingdom</a> and above all to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, which rose at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> in the late 7th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kantoli.jpg" alt="Kantoli" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Nicolaes Visscher II · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolaes_Visscher_Indiae_Orientalis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>441 CE – 563 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gautama Subhadra, Vijayavarman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gantoli, Kan-to-li</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=502&amp;focus=kantoli" aria-label="Kantoli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7521 2207 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kantoli lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7841 2463h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7841 2463h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Indonesia. <a href="/atlas?year=502&amp;focus=kantoli">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

